/* ==========================================================
   macOS Native – Data Hub
   Patched: pt-based typography (was mixing px with the Windows
   file), subtle native-style row hover, explicit selection
   color via palette tokens, message box width, ChartPanel
   hairline restored.
   ========================================================== */

/* ============================================================
   PLATFORM PARITY NOTES (read together with fluent_win11.qss)
   - Standard controls (QPushButton, QLineEdit, QComboBox,
     QSpinBox, QMenu, QRadioButton, QScrollBar) are INTENTIONALLY
     left unstyled here. Qt's macOS style plugin already renders
     these as native Aqua/HIG controls; adding custom QSS would
     fight the OS look instead of matching it. This is the macOS
     equivalent of the Windows file's Fluent overrides — same
     goal (platform-native feel), opposite technique (do less,
     not do differently).
   - The chart tab bar (#chartTabs) and activity rail
     (#activityRail) ARE custom-styled below. Both are bespoke,
     app-specific chrome with no native Qt/macOS counterpart —
     Qt's generic tab bar renders as plain boxy tabs, and a
     QToolButton column has no native macOS analog at all — so
     leaving them unstyled doesn't yield a native look, it just
     yields an unstyled one. The rules are scoped tightly by
     #objectName so other QTabWidgets (e.g. inside dialogs) stay
     fully native.
   - Row hover uses a faint alpha overlay (rgba 0.04), not the
     saturated accent fill used on Windows: macOS list/table
     hover is conventionally subtle, reserving full accent color
     for the actual :selected state.
   - Header captions use muted palette(mid), not full-contrast
     text, matching Apple's HIG caption convention for table
     header labels. Don't "fix" this to match Windows.
   - All sizes use pt, matching how AppKit/HIG specifies type
     sizes (the previous px values were inconsistent with that
     and with the Windows file's own pt convention).
   ============================================================ */

/* ============================================================
   WHAT CHANGED AND WHY (parity pass)
   The Windows sheet styled 102 selectors this file did not, and
   the gap was not all deliberate. Three groups were separated:

   1. Native controls (QPushButton, QLineEdit, QComboBox,
      QSpinBox, QScrollBar, QRadioButton, QMenu): still NOT
      styled here. Qt's macOS plugin draws these as real Aqua
      controls; restyling them is what makes an app look foreign
      on macOS. This stays as it was.
   2. App-specific chrome that has no native counterpart
      (cards, section titles, editor panels, icon-only buttons,
      QToolBox headers, collapsible panels, dialog padding).
      These were simply MISSING, so the role= / card= / iconOnly=
      conventions the Python code sets were silently ignored on
      macOS while working on Windows. They are added below with
      HIG metrics rather than Fluent ones.
   3. Structural containers (QGroupBox, QHeaderView) that need a
      rule to avoid inheriting the wrong background.
   ============================================================ */

/* -------------------------------
   Base
-------------------------------- */

* {
  font-size: 13pt;
}

/* Keep QSS minimal to preserve native macOS look. */

/* -------------------------------
   Typography roles
   The Python code marks labels with role= / muted= / sectionTitle=.
   Sizes follow the HIG type ramp (13pt body, 11pt caption) rather
   than the Windows sheet's values.
-------------------------------- */

QLabel[role="title"] {
  font-size: 17pt;
  font-weight: 600;
}

QLabel[role="subtitle"] {
  font-size: 15pt;
  font-weight: 500;
}

QLabel[role="caption"] {
  font-size: 11pt;
  color: palette(mid);
}

QLabel[muted="true"] {
  color: palette(mid);
}

QLabel[sectionTitle="true"] {
  font-size: 11pt;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: palette(mid);
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  padding: 2px 0 4px 0;
}

/* -------------------------------
   Surfaces
   Cards read as the grouped-box surface used by System Settings
   and the Pages/Keynote inspectors: white fill, hairline border,
   10px radius - the same radius already used by ChartPanel and
   the collapsible panels, so all app surfaces agree.
-------------------------------- */

QFrame[card="true"],
QWidget[card="true"] {
  background: palette(base);
  border: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.08);
  border-radius: 10px;
}

QWidget[editorPanel="true"],
QAbstractItemView[editorPanel="true"] {
  background: palette(base);
  border: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
  border-radius: 8px;
}

QGroupBox {
  background: transparent;
  border: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.08);
  border-radius: 10px;
  margin-top: 10px;
  padding-top: 8px;
}

QGroupBox::title {
  subcontrol-origin: margin;
  subcontrol-position: top left;
  left: 10px;
  padding: 0 4px;
  color: palette(mid);
  font-size: 11pt;
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* -------------------------------
   Icon-only buttons
   Set via the iconOnly property in Python. A native bezel around
   a bare glyph looks wrong in a toolbar, so these become
   borderless squares with the same soft highlight used by the
   activity rail - the macOS toolbar-button idiom.
-------------------------------- */

QAbstractButton[iconOnly="true"] {
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 6px;
  padding: 4px;
  min-width: 28px;
  min-height: 28px;
}

QAbstractButton[iconOnly="true"]:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.06);
}

QAbstractButton[iconOnly="true"]:pressed {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
}

QAbstractButton[iconOnly="true"]:checked {
  background: rgba(@ACCENT_RGB@, 0.16);
}

/* -------------------------------
   Emphasis buttons
   macOS marks the default button by filling it with the accent
   colour, which is exactly what primary= / accent= mean here.
   The bezel, focus ring and metrics stay native.
-------------------------------- */

QPushButton[primary="true"],
QPushButton[accent="true"],
QPushButton#primaryDialogButton {
  background: palette(highlight);
  color: palette(highlighted-text);
  border: 1px solid palette(highlight);
  border-radius: 6px;
  padding: 4px 14px;
  font-weight: 600;
}

QPushButton[primary="true"]:disabled,
QPushButton[accent="true"]:disabled,
QPushButton#primaryDialogButton:disabled {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
  border-color: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
  color: palette(mid);
}

/* -------------------------------
   QToolBox
   Used for the properties inspector and the series-operation
   dialogs. Qt draws its headers as bezeled buttons on macOS,
   which fights the flat inspector look; these become flat,
   taller header strips (todo.txt: "QToolBox: increase header
   size") with a faint selected tint.
-------------------------------- */

QToolBox {
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
}

QToolBox::tab {
  text-align: left;
  padding: 12px 14px;
  min-height: 26px;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.04);
  font-weight: 600;
  color: palette(text);
}

QToolBox::tab:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.07);
}

QToolBox::tab:selected {
  background: rgba(@ACCENT_RGB@, 0.12);
  color: palette(text);
}

QToolBox::tab:disabled {
  color: palette(mid);
}

/* -------------------------------
   Dialog padding
   Root layouts go through apply_dialog_shell(), which owns the
   margins; this only guarantees a floor for dialogs Qt builds
   itself (file pickers, message boxes).
-------------------------------- */

QDialog {
  background: palette(window);
}

/* -------------------------------
   Chart panel
-------------------------------- */

QFrame#ChartPanel {
  background: palette(window);
  /* Flush, like Finder's content area.  The chart pane is the
     document, not a card placed on top of one: the tab strip's
     hairline above it and the sidebar's hairline beside it are the
     only edges it needs, and a rounded border inside a tab pane is
     the detail that most gives a Qt app away on macOS. */
  border: none;
  border-radius: 0;
}

/* Palette tokens, not literal white/black: hard-coded colours make the
   toolbar unreadable in macOS dark mode. */
QToolBar {
  /*background: transparent;
  color: palette(text);*/
  border: none;
  spacing: 2px;
}

QToolBar::separator {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
  width: 1px;
  margin: 4px 6px;
}

QToolButton#chartToolButton {
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 6px;
  padding: 3px;
}

QToolButton#chartToolButton:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.06);
}

QToolButton#chartToolButton:checked {
  background: rgba(@ACCENT_RGB@, 0.16);
}

/* "Adatta" (zoom_fit) is a plain QToolButton built by create_toolbar_button,
   not one of the QToolBar's own actions, so it never picked up the
   #chartToolButton objectName the loop in ChartPanel._configure_toolbar
   assigns. Left unstyled, it fell through to the native Aqua QToolButton
   bevel, which looks out of place sitting flat against #chartToolButton's
   borderless siblings in the same row. Same flat/hover treatment here keeps
   the whole top row visually consistent. */
QToolButton#zoom_fitButton {
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 6px;
  padding: 3px;
}

QToolButton#zoom_fitButton:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.06);
}

/* -------------------------------
   Tabs — Finder document tabs.

   Qt's macOS style draws segmented-control tabs, which is the
   right look for a *settings* tab bar but not for a document tab
   bar like Finder's or Safari's.  Finder tabs are: a full-width
   strip the height of a toolbar row, tabs separated by single
   hairlines with no gap and no rounded pills, the selected tab
   reading as the window surface continuing upwards while the rest
   sit on a slightly recessed bar, and a hairline closing the strip
   underneath.

   These rules are intentionally NOT scoped to #chartTabs: applying
   one tab look to every QTabWidget is what makes the chart tabs
   and the axis-properties tabs agree, which is the point.
-------------------------------- */

QTabWidget::pane {
  border: none;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.12);
  top: -1px;
}

QTabBar {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.05);
  qproperty-drawBase: 0;
}

QTabBar::tab {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.05);
  color: palette(mid);
  border: none;
  /* Finder separates tabs with a single hairline rather than
     spacing them apart, so the divider is a right border and the
     tabs sit flush against each other. */
  border-right: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.12);
  /* 13pt text in a 28px strip is the Finder tab height.  Padding
     is horizontal-only so the strip height stays constant however
     long the title is. */
  padding: 6px 16px;
  margin: 0;
  /* Deliberately small.  A large min-width would put a floor under
     the whole chart pane, which is exactly the negotiation the tab
     bar was taken out of (see _configure_tabs). */
  min-width: 60px;
}

QTabBar::tab:first {
  border-left: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.12);
}

QTabBar::tab:hover:!selected {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.09);
  color: palette(text);
}

QTabBar::tab:selected {
  /* The selected tab is the window surface continuing upwards. */
  background: palette(base);
  color: palette(text);
  font-weight: 500;
}

QTabBar::tab:only-one {
  border-left: none;
  border-right: none;
}

QTabBar::close-button {
  subcontrol-position: right;
  margin: 0 2px;
}

/* The scroll arrows that appear when the titles do not fit: Finder
   shows them as bare chevrons on the strip, not as bezelled
   buttons. */
QTabBar QToolButton {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.05);
  border: none;
  border-left: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.12);
}

QTabBar QToolButton:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.09);
}
/* -------------------------------
   Activity rail (#activityRail)
   A narrow icon sidebar has no native macOS widget to defer to,
   so this gets explicit treatment: a faint tint to read as a
   distinct chrome strip (like Mail/Finder sidebars), tool
   buttons as borderless squares that pick up a soft rounded
   highlight on hover, and a tinted accent capsule for the
   active section — matching the translucent (not solid-fill)
   selection look of macOS sidebar items.
-------------------------------- */

QFrame#activityRail {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.025);
  border: none;
  border-right: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.08);
}

QToolButton#activityButton {
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 6px;
}

QToolButton#activityButton:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.06);
}

QToolButton#activityButton:checked {
  background: rgba(@ACCENT_RGB@, 0.16);
}

QToolButton#activityButton:checked:hover {
  background: rgba(@ACCENT_RGB@, 0.22);
}

QToolButton#activityButton::menu-indicator {
  image: none;
}

/* -------------------------------
   Chart navigation toolbar (#chartToolbar)
   NavigationToolbar2QT is built by Matplotlib, not us — its
   buttons, separators, and coordinate label come in as bare,
   unstyled Qt controls (native bezel buttons, a grooved
   separator line, plain label text). Tagged in chart_panel.py
   so they can be flattened to match the rest of the app's
   chrome: borderless icon buttons, translucent accent capsule
   for the active Pan/Zoom tool (same recipe as the activity
   rail), a hairline separator instead of a native groove, and
   a muted caption-sized coordinate readout.
-------------------------------- */

QLabel#chartCoordLabel {
  color: palette(mid);
  font-size: 11pt;
  padding: 0 6px;
}

/* -------------------------------
   Properties split control
   Each CollapsiblePanel reads as a single rounded card (same
   language as ChartPanel/TableListPanel below), with a flat
   disclosure-triangle header — like the Format inspector in
   Pages/Keynote — instead of a bezeled push button. The toggle
   button is set autoRaise=True in Python so macOS doesn't draw
   its native button bezel/font over this; the rules below just
   add the rounded-card chrome and hover feedback.
-------------------------------- */

QFrame#collapsiblePanel {
  background: palette(base);
  border: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.08);
  border-radius: 10px;
}

QToolButton#panelToggleButton {
  text-align: left;
  padding: 8px 10px;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: transparent;
  font-weight: 600;
}

QToolButton#panelToggleButton:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.05);
}

QToolButton#panelToggleButton:pressed {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.09);
}

QWidget#panelBody {
  background: transparent;
}

/* -------------------------------
   The sidebar — Finder's, not a card.

   This is the largest departure from the Windows sheet, and it is
   deliberate.  Finder's sidebar is not a panel sitting inside the
   window: it *is* the left edge of the window.  It has no border,
   no corner radius and no card fill; it is a faintly recessed
   surface separated from the content by a single hairline, and its
   selection is a rounded capsule inset from both edges rather than
   a full-bleed accent row.

   Object-name rules, so they win over the QWidget[card="true"]
   block above: the Python code builds these with the same
   create_card_widget() helper as every other surface, and on
   Windows a card is the right answer.
-------------------------------- */

QWidget#leftPanelCard,
QWidget#dataPageCard,
QFrame#leftPanelCard,
QFrame#dataPageCard {
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 0;
}

QWidget#leftPanelCard {
  /* Faintly recessed against the content, which is what makes the
     sidebar read as a sidebar without drawing a box around it. */
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.02);
  /* The hairline that divides sidebar from content.  The splitter
     handle sits on top of it and is transparent, so the line does
     not double up while dragging. */
  border-right: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
}

/* Finder's divider is a hairline you aim at, not a visible bar. */
QSplitter::handle {
  background: transparent;
}

QSplitter::handle:horizontal {
  width: 6px;
}

QSplitter::handle:vertical {
  height: 6px;
  /* The one divider that is drawn: between the file list and the
     preview underneath it, as in a Finder column split. */
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.08);
}

/* -------------------------------
   TableListPanel — the sidebar list

   No frame, no grid, no alternating rows: a Finder sidebar is a
   plain list on the sidebar surface.  Selection is the capsule.
-------------------------------- */

TableListPanel QTableView {
  background: transparent;
  color: palette(text);
  border: none;
  border-radius: 0;
  gridline-color: transparent;
  /* Finder's sidebar rows are taller than a data grid's. */
  show-decoration-selected: 1;
  /* The capsule below is the only thing that paints a selected row.  Left at
     the default, the view fills the whole row rect with the highlight colour
     and the capsule sits on top of it, so the 6px margin either side shows as
     a second, darker blue. */
  selection-background-color: transparent;
  selection-color: palette(highlighted-text);
}

TableListPanel QTableView::item {
  padding: 4px 8px;
  border-radius: 6px;
  margin: 1px 6px;
}

/* Subtle native-style hover: a faint wash, not an accent fill */
TableListPanel QTableView::item:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.05);
}

/* One colour across the whole row: every cell of the selected row gets the
   same capsule, so the name column and the columns beside it read as one
   selection rather than a light field inside a darker band. */
TableListPanel QTableView::item:selected,
TableListPanel QTableView::item:selected:active,
TableListPanel QTableView::item:selected:!active {
  background: palette(highlight);
  color: palette(highlighted-text);
}

/* Finder's sidebar section captions: small, semibold, muted, and
   *not* uppercase - "Favorites", not "FAVORITES". */
TableListPanel QHeaderView::section {
  background: transparent;
  color: palette(mid);
  font-size: 11pt;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0;
  padding: 4px 10px;
  border: none;
}

/* -------------------------------
   TablePreviewPanel — the file list

   The content side of Finder: alternating rows, no vertical grid,
   a flat header with hairline dividers that reads as a column
   header rather than as a button strip.
-------------------------------- */

TablePreviewPanel QTableView {
  background: palette(base);
  alternate-background-color: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.03);
  color: palette(text);
  border: none;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.08);
  border-radius: 0;
  gridline-color: transparent;
  /* Same fix as TableListPanel above, and for the same reason: left at the
     default, Aqua paints the whole row rect with the highlight colour and
     ::item:selected's background paints again on top of it, so a selected
     row here read as a visibly different, two-tone blue next to
     TableListPanel's flat one - the two tables looked selection-inconsistent
     side by side even though both QSS blocks were "styled". */
  selection-background-color: transparent;
  selection-color: palette(highlighted-text);
}

TablePreviewPanel QTableView::item {
  padding: 2px 8px;
}

TablePreviewPanel QTableView::item:hover {
  background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.04);
}

TablePreviewPanel QTableView::item:selected {
  background: palette(highlight);
  color: palette(highlighted-text);
}

TablePreviewPanel QHeaderView::section {
  background: palette(window);
  color: palette(mid);
  font-size: 11pt;
  font-weight: 500;
  letter-spacing: 0;
  padding: 4px 8px;
  border: none;
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
  border-right: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.06);
}

TablePreviewPanel QHeaderView::section:last {
  border-right: none;
}

/* -------------------------------
   Message boxes / dialogs
   Same Qt-wide wrapping issue as Windows, not platform-specific
-------------------------------- */

QMessageBox QLabel {
  min-width: 0px;
  max-width: 360px;
}

/* -------------------------------
   Log viewer
   A log is columns, not prose: monospaced, not wrapped, and inset
   in a card so the text does not run into the window edge.  The
   fixed-width font is set in Python (the system fixed font differs
   per platform); this only supplies the surface.
-------------------------------- */

QPlainTextEdit#logViewerText {
  background: palette(base);
  border: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
  border-radius: 8px;
  padding: 6px 8px;
  selection-background-color: palette(highlight);
}

/* -------------------------------
   Chart notes pane
   The report a series operation leaves under its chart.  A hairline
   above it, and nothing else: it is part of the chart tab, not a
   card floating inside one.
-------------------------------- */

HtmlResultsView#chartNotesView {
  background: palette(base);
  border: none;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.10);
}

/* ==========================================================
   Fluent 2 / macOS Command List
   SeriesOperationWidget
   ========================================================== */

/* Command rows */

QFrame#operationRow {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

/* Fluent hover */

QFrame#operationRow:hover {
    background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.045);
}

/* Pressed */

QFrame#operationRow:pressed {
    background: rgba(@SHADOW_RGB@, 0.08);
}

/* Keyboard focus */

QFrame#operationRow:focus {
    background: rgba(@ACCENT_RGB@, 0.05);
    border-left: 3px solid palette(highlight);
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   Section headers
   Styled once already, up in "Typography roles" (11pt,
   palette(mid), same uppercase/letter-spacing treatment). This
   block used to redeclare the selector at 9pt with a hardcoded
   rgba() grey copied straight from fluent_win11.qss - a size no
   other caption in this file uses, and a colour that doesn't
   track the palette the way palette(mid) does. Removed rather
   than "converted": the earlier rule already renders this label
   correctly, so a second definition only risked drifting out of
   sync with it again.
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   Command title
   HIG list rows keep the primary label at body size in the
   normal text colour; only the selection state (below) gets an
   accent tint. palette(highlight) here was copied from the
   Windows sheet's identical block without updating it - Windows
   uses palette(window-text) - so every row title rendered in the
   accent blue, permanently, like a hyperlink. That is the "not
   very macOS" look: fixed to plain text colour at the file's
   13pt body size instead of the Fluent list's denser 10pt.
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */

QLabel[operationTitle="true"] {

    font-size: 13pt;

    font-weight: 600;

    color: palette(text);

    background: transparent;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   Description
   Same copy-paste issue as the title: a hardcoded rgba() grey
   and a 9pt size, instead of the palette(mid) + 11pt caption
   convention every other secondary label in this file uses.
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */

QLabel[operationDescription="true"] {

    background: transparent;

    color: palette(mid);

    font-size: 11pt;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   Operation Icon
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */

QLabel#operationIcon {

    min-width: 20px;
    max-width: 20px;

    min-height: 20px;
    max-height: 20px;

    background: transparent;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   Compact row variant
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */

QFrame#operationRow[compact="true"] {

    border-radius: 6px;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   Selected operation
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */

QFrame#operationRow[selected="true"] {

    background: rgba(@ACCENT_RGB@, 0.08);

    border-left: 3px solid palette(highlight);
}
`